  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 106 PM CDT Tue Aug 18 2026\n\nTHIS AFTERNOON: A weak frontal boundary continues to sink southward \nearly this afternoon, with noticeable strato-cu field \ndeveloping along the Highway 20 corridor. The remainder of the \nafternoon is likely to remain dry in most locations, outside of \nvery isolated development along this aforementioned boundary. \n\nTHIS EVENING/TONIGHT: Some guidance shows isolated development this \nevening along a weak convergence zone in SW Minnesota and northern \nIowa. This convection may also be sparked by a weak area of \nvorticity dropping through the Dakotas from the northwest. Any storm \nthat develops will be in an area of MLCAPE AOA 1000-1500 J/KG, with \nsufficient shear to promote perhaps a rotating storm.\nAnticipating the severe weather risks to remain low. \n\nFurther west, additional convection is expected to form over the \nBlack Hill region this evening as a stronger wave enters the area. \nThe track of this wave, along with how the LLJ propagates overnight, \nshould push some of this activity into south central SD after 1am. \nMUCAPE remains quite low, with marginal mid-lvl lapse rates, so \nwidespread severe weather is not anticipated.  Though, a few gusty \nwind gusts could remain possible through 3am. There remains \nreasonable agreement that this convection could track along the MO \nriver valley into eastern Nebraska by daybreak.  This is an area \nwhere higher PoPs will be focused. \n\nWEDNESDAY: The departure of this shortwave should lead to a period \nof subsidence and quiet weather well into Wednesday. Cloud cover may \nbe more substancial over the southern half of the CWA thanks to mid-\nupr clouds from convection. By the afternoon southwesterly surface \nwinds will return, allowing high temperatures to rise into the upper \n70s to lower 80s. Convection risks in the afternoon and evening may \nbe quite narrowed to a zone ahead of an approaching surface front in \nnorth central SD.  Further southeast, there doesn't seem to be any \nstrong focusing mechanism, with weak convective inhibition likely \nkeeping the area free of any isolated larger storm growth. While \nsurface and low-lvl focus remains absent, we'll need to watch the \npresence of any weak vorticity in the NW flow. Further northwest,\nany convection that develops along the frontal boundary may try to\ntrack southeast, though lessening instability should limit the \noverall severe risks. \n\nTHURSDAY: Any precipitation risk continues to look minimal heading \ninto Thursday morning as a stronger EML begins to build into the \nregion.  Southwesterly surface winds overnight may keep overnight \nlow temperatures from falling significantly. The aforementioned\nsurface boundary from Wednesday will sink southeast through the\nday on Thursday providing a focus mechanism for storms by late \nafternoon and evening. MLCAPE approaching 2000-3000 J/KG may develop\nalong this boundary as dew points try to quickly surge northward.\nThe lingering questions for convective development are focused \non the track of mid-lvl vorticity in the late afternoon, along with\nconvective inhibition ahead of the boundary. The track of shortwave\nenergy could favor more development in central and northern Minnesota,\nwith only isolated storms further southwest along the boundary. While\ninstability is greater, shear is a bit more marginal suggesting\nless organization to anything that develops.\n\n\nFRIDAY-MONDAY: Mid-lvl heights begin to rise over the Rockies as the \nridge amplifies. The lingering, but sharper, NW flow pattern \nover the Dakotas may bring one more wave into the region Friday.\nHowever the southward passage of Thursdays front should push \nthe best boundary layer moisture south of the area. The ridge \naxis begins to push eastward as we move into the upcoming \nweekend. A pleasant Saturday is likely in the region with highs \neither side of 80. Increasing low-lvl warm advection into Sunday\ncould bring elevated showers and thunderstorms eastward into \nSunday morning, along with more clouds and slightly warmer highs\ninto the lower to middle 80s. At this time, rain coverage may \nbe limited with only a 20-30% PoP.",
